# TEXT: string ordering, declared-`main` twin of `table/str/order.id`.
# Byte-lexicographic over the UNSIGNED byte domain, in the process's initial
# C locale.
#
# Three rows: "Z" against "a" (uppercase below lowercase), a prefix against
# its extension, and the discriminating non-ASCII row. "é" is 0xC3 0xA9 and
# "z" is 0x7A, so the leading byte decides: 195 sorts ABOVE 122 and `t < u`
# is false. Read as SIGNED char, 0xC3 is -61 and the comparison inverts — the
# third row is the only one that tells the two readings apart.
#
# THE UNSIGNED DOMAIN IS NOT INFERRED FROM THE COMPILER. `str/bytes.id`, an
# oracle in this same corpus that AGREES on both realizations, records the
# bytes of "é" as 195 and 169. An ordering over that same byte domain cannot
# place 195 below 122. `law.md` §0 and §8 make ordinary Lua meaning the entry
# of the specialization chain, and Lua 5.4 §3.4.4 orders strings by locale
# collation; under the initial C locale that is unsigned-char order, which is
# what the generated `strcmp` performs. Codepoint order agrees (U+00E9 >
# U+007A).
#
# SCOPE: this fixture pins the C-locale answer only. Lua ordering follows the
# CURRENT locale and this tree exposes `os.setlocale`, while generated
# comparisons call `strcmp` unconditionally — so a non-C locale is a real
# divergence this oracle does not cover and does not license.
# expect: 1 1 0
main: i64 = ()
  p = "Z"
  q = "a"
  a = 0
  if p < q
    a = 1
  r = "ab"
  s = "abc"
  b = 0
  if r < s
    b = 1
  t = "é"
  u = "z"
  c = 0
  if t < u
    c = 1
  print("{a} {b} {c}")
  0
